Package: clevo-control-dkms
Section: kernel
Architecture: all
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
Conflicts:
 tuxedo-drivers,
 tuxedo-keyboard-dkms,
Suggests:
 clevo-control,
Description: Clevo laptop keyboard backlight, Fn keys and performance profiles (DKMS)
 Kernel module for Clevo laptops that exposes, through standard kernel
 interfaces:
 .
  * the RGB keyboard backlight as the LED rgb:kbd_backlight;
  * the backlight Fn keys (brightness, on/off, next color);
  * the firmware performance profiles as a platform profile, so that the
    desktop power mode also switches the firmware profile.
 .
 The module needs Linux 6.14 or later and is built with DKMS.

Package: clevo-control
Architecture: all
Depends:
 gir1.2-ayatanaappindicator3-0.1,
 gir1.2-gtk-3.0,
 gir1.2-notify-0.7,
 python3-gi,
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${python3:Depends},
Recommends:
 clevo-control-dkms,
 power-profiles-daemon,
Suggests:
 gnome-shell-extension-clevo-control,
Description: tools for the keyboard backlight and performance profile of Clevo laptops
 Userspace companions of the clevo-control kernel module:
 .
  * clevoctl, a command line tool for the keyboard backlight color and
    brightness and for the firmware performance profile;
  * clevo-control-tray, a tray applet with a color chooser, a brightness
    slider and a profile menu;
  * a device rule and a service that let users change these settings without
    root and remember the backlight color across reboots.

Package: gnome-shell-extension-clevo-control
Section: gnome
Architecture: all
Depends:
 gnome-shell (<< 51~),
 gnome-shell (>= 50~),
 ${misc:Depends},
Recommends:
 clevo-control,
 clevo-control-dkms,
 power-profiles-daemon,
Enhances:
 gnome-shell,
Description: Quiet performance profile of Clevo laptops in the GNOME power menu
 GNOME Shell extension that adds a "Quiet" entry to the Power Mode menu of the
 quick settings. It selects the firmware performance profile with the lowest
 power limits, which the three power modes of the desktop cannot reach.
 .
 It needs the clevo-control kernel module and the device rule from the
 clevo-control package.
